%[text] %[text:anchor:T_9DC2AB1A] # Modeling Traffic
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text] As nearly every driver in a city has experienced, traffic jams are a common feature of modern life. While in some cases, a clear cause of bad weather or an earlier accident can be apparent, in many cases, there is no apparent reason why traffic seems to progress in fits and starts. Changing the behavior of humans is difficult, but perhaps we can change the behavior of cars to mitigate the problems. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A traffic jam on a freeway with men standing on the road selling bread](text:image:322f)
%[text]{"align":"center"} A photograph of Istanbul traffic by [Reise Reise](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Otoyol_2_Richtung_FSM_2.jpg), [CC BY-SA 3.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/deed.en)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] Modeling traffic as a partial differential equation will allow us to see how traffic jams appear and dissipate. 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6bd7] ## Notes on these solutions
%[text] This script 'unrolls' the contents of the TrafficModel.m script so that each possible option selected can have its corresponding results and output shown. Thus, there are repeated sections in this script where the differences are in which values are selected in the live controls.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ACF37C64] ## Continuity Equations
%[text] One way to describe transport equations is by how they relate to conservation laws. If we require that a quantity is *locally* conserved, we can describe its motion as a continuous flow. This is useful for many physical systems that we conceive as flowing, such as gases or liquids, or even, in a modeling sense, traffic. Fundamentally, we will describe a control region (in one dimension this is just a line segment) from $x=0${"altText":"x=0"} to $x=L${"altText":"x=L"}. Let's consider the quantity of momentum, the product of mass density $\\rho${"altText":"ρ"} and flow velocity $\\vec{u}${"altText":"vector u"}.  As we observe the system over time, there can be flow into and out of the system through the ends of our domain at $x=0${"altText":"x=0"} and $x=L${"altText":"x=L"}. This corresponds to cars entering or leaving the region of study at various speeds and is called the flux, which is the momentum flowing per unit of time through the point. If $\\vec{u}(x,t)${"altText":"vector u(x,t)"} is the velocity of the cars at point $x${"altText":"x"} and time $t${"altText":"t"}, then the mass flux at that point is $\\vec{j}(x,t) = \\rho(x,t)\\left(\\vec{u}(x,t)\\right)^2${"altText":"vector j(x,t) = ρ(x,t)(vector u(x,t))^2"}. We could also include a source term (roadway onramps) and a sink term (roadway exits), but let's begin with a simple closed system, such as a professional car race with a fixed number of competitors driving on a closed course. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial (\\rho u)}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} \\left(\\rho u^2\\right) = 0${"altText":"(∂ (ρ u))\/(∂ t) + (∂)\/(∂ x) (ρ u^2) = 0"}
%[text] Applying the product rule, this is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t} + \\rho\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} (\\rho u) + \\rho u \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 0${"altText":"u(∂ ρ)\/(∂ t) + ρ(∂ u)\/(∂ t) + u(∂)\/(∂ x) (ρ u) + ρ u (∂ u)\/(∂ x) = 0"}
%[text] which simplifies to 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u \\left(\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t} +\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} (\\rho u)}\_{\\text{conservation of mass}}\\right) +  \\rho\\left(\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} +  u \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}}\_{\\text{1-D nonlinear transport}}\\right) = 0${"altText":"u ((∂ ρ)\/(∂ t) +(∂)\/(∂ x) (ρ u)\_(conservation of mass)) + ρ((∂ u)\/(∂ t) + u (∂ u)\/(∂ x)\_(1-D nonlinear transport)) = 0"}.
%[text] However, because mass is conserved, the first term is 0, and since a vehicle's mass density should be non-zero, we can solve the 1-D transport equation we recognize. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3F1DE96E] ### Adding Dimensions
%[text] In higher dimensions, the dimensions of the continuity equation also go up, such as the momentum per unit volume, $\\rho(x,y,z,t)\\vec{u}(x,y,z,t)${"altText":"ρ(x,y,z,t)vector u(x,y,z,t)"}, and the flux $\\vec{j} = \\rho (\\vec{u}\\cdot \\vec{u})${"altText":"vector j = ρ (vector u · vector u)"} is the momentum per unit area per unit time.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8310C93B] ## Nonlinear Transport Equation
%[text] Consider a simple nonlinear, homogeneous partial differential wave equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_7435E9CD] $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 0${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) + u(∂ u)\/(∂ x) = 0"}.       $(\\diamondsuit)${"altText":"(diamond suit)"}
%[text] In this nonlinear equation, however, the transport speed is not uniform but depends on the wave function. The[ characteristic curve method](file:../../Scripts/MethodOfCharacteristics.m) does allow an approach to solving this equation, although it may be complicated to express the solution analytically. Remember that we assume the curves we are solving for are contour lines of the solution, so the value $u(X(s),T(s))=u(x\_0,t\_0)=u\_0${"altText":"u(X(s),T(s))=u(x\_0,t\_0)=u\_0"} along each curve. This means that the value of $u${"altText":"u"} is a constant along each characteristic curve, which reduces [Equation $(\\diamondsuit)${"altText":"(diamond suit)"}](internal:M_7435E9CD) to a (potentially different) constant speed wave equation along trajectories passing through each initial value. 
%[text] ![](text:image:1f15) **Try**. What do these characteristic plots look like with different initial values?
tic
