%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7684AF99] ## Locating and Resolving Rarefaction Regions
%[text] A rarefaction region occurs when a region in the positive time characteristic plot does not have any characteristic curves passing through it. This corresponds to the example in [MethodOfCharacteristics](file:../../Scripts/MethodOfCharacteristics.m:H_0FEC8625): Nonlinear Transport Equation with a step up. Recall that the solution corresponding to a characteristic plot with an (unresolved) rarefaction region looks like:
  %[control:button:8cd4]{"position":[1,2]}
AnimateRarefaction(-1,2) %[output:4f3a87a4]
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:83c5) **Exercise 1.** For each initial condition above, check the corresponding box if the characteristic plot displays a rarefaction region:
Gaussian = false; Trigonometric = false; Sinusoidal = false; GreenLight = true; RedLight = false; %[control:checkbox:7726]{"position":[12,17]} %[control:checkbox:75b9]{"position":[35,40]} %[control:checkbox:57d5]{"position":[55,60]} %[control:checkbox:5417]{"position":[75,79]} %[control:checkbox:9c38]{"position":[92,97]}
  %[control:button:3d7d]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckExercise1(Gaussian,Trigonometric,Sinusoidal,GreenLight,RedLight,jumppoint,abs(speed)) % Trying to add plot %[output:046cabc6] %[output:07892970] %[output:21c38c13]
%[text] A rarefaction will always have a point $(t\_{\\star},x\_{\\star})${"altText":"(t\_(star),x\_(star))"} where the region begins. We can also define the characteristic curves that bound the region as $x\\text{Plus}(t)${"altText":"xPlus(t)"} giving the upper boundary and $x\\text{Minus}(t)${"altText":"xMinus(t)"} giving the lower boundary.
%%
%[text]  ![](text:image:4241) **Exercise 2.** 
