%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DC73F2BA] ## Locating Shock Regions
%[text] Say $u = f(x\_0)${"altText":"u = f(x\_0)"} along characteristic curves. A shock occurs in the region where our characteristic curves predict multiple values of the solution. To define the shock region, we need to locate where the shock starts. Then, we will be able to propagate the shock forward in time. The shock occurs when the slope of the solution becomes undefined, as at a piecewise discontinuity or by approaching $-\\infty${"altText":"-infinity"}. Recall the first-order time-dependent PDE:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 0${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) + u(∂ u)\/(∂ x) = 0"} $(\\diamond)${"altText":"(diamond)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $u(x,t\_0) = f(x)${"altText":"u(x,t\_0) = f(x)"}
%[text] Let's consider the function $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x\_0}\\frac{\\partial x\_0}{\\partial x}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ x) = (df)\/(dx\_0)(∂ x\_0)\/(∂ x)"}. We have computed that $x\_0=x-tu${"altText":"x\_0=x-tu"}, so we can compute 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial x\_0}{\\partial x}=1-t\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}${"altText":"(∂ x\_0)\/(∂ x)=1-t(∂ u)\/(∂ x)"} 
%[text] so 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\left(1+t f'(x\_0)\\right) = f'(x\_0)${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ x)(1+t f'(x\_0)) = f'(x\_0)"} $\\Rightarrow${"altText":"implies"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{f'(x\_0)}{1+tf'(x\_0)}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ x) = (f'(x\_0))\/(1+tf'(x\_0))"}.  
%[text] This rate of change will blow up (diverge to infinity) if $tf'(x\_0) \\to -1${"altText":"tf'(x\_0) approaches -1"}, or in other words, as $t \\to -\\frac{1}{f'(x\_0)}${"altText":"t approaches -(1)\/(f'(x\_0))"}. Thus, we locate the shock discontinuity at the earliest time this value is achieved:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $t\_{\\star} = \\min\\left\\{-\\frac{1}{f'(y)}\~|\~f'(y)\<0\\right\\}${"altText":"t\_(star) = min(-(1)\/(f'(y)) | f'(y)\<0)"}
%[text] and, if $y\_{\\star}${"altText":"y\_(star)"} is the value such that $t\_{\\star} = -\\frac{1}{f'(y\_{\\star})}${"altText":"t\_(star) = -(1)\/(f'(y\_(star)))"}, then $x\_{\\star} = y\_{\\star} + f(y\_{\\star})t\_{\\star}${"altText":"x\_(star) = y\_(star) + f(y\_(star))t\_(star)"}.
%%
%[text]  ![](text:image:0f0f) **Exercise 3 (red light).** Select an initial condition that exhibits a shock and compute the starting point for the shock. 
jumppoint = -1; %[text:anchor:M_45F4404F]
speed = 2;
f(x,t) = piecewise(x<jumppoint,abs(speed),0); IC="red"; %[control:dropdown:1876]{"position":[10,55]}
t0 = 0;
syms x
ICf(x) = f(x,t0);
displayFormula("f(x) == ICf") %[output:959e0a29]
  %[control:button:8b3e]{"position":[1,2]}
[~,xEqnShock,uSolShock] = SolveCharacteristicEquationNonlinear(f,1,u,0,t0,PrintSoln=false);
tValues = [t0:0.05:3 t0:-0.05:-1];
xValues = -11:0.15:7;
xLimits = [-5 7];
PlotCharacteristicCurves(tValues,xValues,ICf,xEqnShock,xLimits,NumColors=20,animate=false); %[control:checkbox:3353]{"position":[85,90]} %[output:8293848b]
%[text] 
%[text] **Note:** Some choices generate much easier calculations than others. If you are struggling with the computations, try a different shock.
%%
yStar = -1; %[control:editfield:4be2]{"position":[9,11]}
tStar = 0; %[control:editfield:7468]{"position":[9,10]}
xStar = -1; %[control:editfield:71b0]{"position":[9,11]}
PassedEx3 = CheckExercise3(yStar,tStar,xStar,IC,ICf,jumppoint); %[output:80539ab3]
  %[control:button:12d8]{"position":[1,2]}
